translate english noche_0001:
    c_narrator "A lofty-arched, narrow, Gothic chamber. FAUST, in a chair at his desk, restless."

translate english noche_0002:
    voice "voice/noche_0002.ogg"
    c_faust "I’ve studied now Philosophy\nAnd Jurisprudence, Medicine,—\nAnd even, alas! Theology,—\nFrom end to end, with labor keen;"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "And here, poor fool! with all my lore\nI stand, no wiser than before:\nI’m Magister—yea, Doctor—hight,\nAnd straight or cross-wise, wrong or right,"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "These ten years long, with many woes,\nI’ve led my scholars by the nose,—\nAnd see, that nothing can be known!\nThat knowledge cuts me to the bone."
    voice sustain
    c_faust "I’m cleverer, true, than those fops of teachers,\nDoctors and Magisters, Scribes and Preachers;\nNeither scruples nor doubts come now to smite me,\nNor Hell nor Devil can longer affright me."
    voice sustain
    c_faust "For this, all pleasure am I foregoing;\nI do not pretend to aught worth knowing,\nI do not pretend I could be a teacher\nTo help or convert a fellow-creature."
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Then, too, I’ve neither lands nor gold,\nNor the world’s least pomp or honor hold—\nNo dog would endure such a curst existence!\nWherefore, from Magic I seek assistance,"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "That many a secret perchance I reach\nThrough spirit-power and spirit-speech,\nAnd thus the bitter task forego\nOf saying the things I do not know,—"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "That I may detect the inmost force\nWhich binds the world, and guides its course;\nIts germs, productive powers explore,\nAnd rummage in empty words no more!"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "O full and splendid Moon, whom I\nHave, from this desk, seen climb the sky\nSo many a midnight,—would thy glow\nFor the last time beheld my woe!"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Ever thine eye, most mournful friend,\nO’er books and papers saw me bend;\nBut would that I, on mountains grand,\nAmid thy blessed light could stand,"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "With spirits through mountain-caverns hover,\nFloat in thy twilight the meadows over,\nAnd, freed from the fumes of lore that swathe me,\nTo health in thy dewy fountains bathe me!"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Ah, me! this dungeon still I see.\nThis drear, accursed masonry,\nWhere even the welcome daylight strains\nBut duskly through the painted panes."
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Hemmed in by many a toppling heap\nOf books worm-eaten, gray with dust,\nWhich to the vaulted ceiling creep,\nAgainst the smoky paper thrust,—"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "With glasses, boxes, round me stacked,\nAnd instruments together hurled,\nAncestral lumber, stuffed and packed—\nSuch is my world: and what a world!"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "And do I ask, wherefore my heart\nFalters, oppressed with unknown needs?\nWhy some inexplicable smart\nAll movement of my life impedes?"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Alas! in living Nature’s stead,\nWhere God His human creature set,\nIn smoke and mould the fleshless dead\nAnd bones of beasts surround me yet!"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Fly! Up, and seek the broad, free land!\nAnd this one Book of Mystery\nFrom Nostradamus’ very hand,\nIs’t not sufficient company?"
    voice sustain
    c_faust "When I the starry courses know,\nAnd Nature’s wise instruction seek,\nWith light of power my soul shall glow,\nAs when to spirits spirits speak."
    voice sustain
    c_faust "Tis vain, this empty brooding here,\nThough guessed the holy symbols be:\nYe, Spirits, come—ye hover near—\nOh, if you hear me, answer me!"

